<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ōmura Masujirō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1824–1869</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Omura Masujiro, Murata Zoroku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ōmura Masujirō, born Murata Zōroku, was a Japanese physician and scholar of Western learning who became the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s modern army. He served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a> domain, where he helped build a steamship, and later the Chōshū domain, whose forces he reorganised and led to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> in 1866. As vice-minister of military affairs in the Meiji government he planned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army, but was assassinated in 1869.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omura-masujiro.jpg" alt="Ōmura Masujirō" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edoardo Chiossone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masujiro_Omura_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
